      "id": 1,
      "text": "Brushton, New York, was officially named on October 1, 1877, after Henry Brush, a significant landowner in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brushton%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village of Brushton is located on the Little Salmon River, which played a crucial role in its early industrial development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brushton%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Henry Brush, who founded Brushton, was also known for his engaging public speaking and significant contributions to local industrial development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brushton%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Brushton is situated in Franklin County, New York, within the eastern part of the town of Moira.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brushton%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community of Brushton is primarily agricultural, with a history of industrial enterprises dating back to its early settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brushton%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Brushton features two small unincorporated villages, including itself and Moira, both served by the Rutland Railroad.",
